@meta
id: 30-15
title: Scenario — the conflicted merge
chapter: 30
type: scenario
skills: git-in-vim, global-delete, search-slash, arglist
par: 137
minutes: 14
vscode: the three-way merge editor, and its Accept Both button
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts src/domain/order/order.entity.ts MERGE_MSG

@brief
A ticket, of the kind that arrives at five to six.

    You pulled main into feature/refund-window. Two files conflict.
    Resolve them, and write the merge message.

Vim is already git's editor: when a merge stops, git leaves the
conflicted files marked up and opens this exact kind of buffer for the
message. Nothing here needs git to be running — a conflict is text, and
text is the thing you are good at now.

  1. `order.provider.ts` — both sides are right. Main added the guard
     that refuses to cancel a shipped order. This branch added the
     thirty day refund window. The method needs both, main's guard
     first, with a blank line between the two blocks.

  2. `order.entity.ts` — main wins. `cancellationReason` is the name
     the rest of the service already uses; this branch's
     `cancelReason` was never agreed. Take main's side and drop this
     branch's.

  3. `MERGE_MSG` — under the subject line, and separated from it by the
     blank line that is already there, record what you did:

         Kept the shipped guard from main and the refund window here.

     Leave the commented lines below it alone. Git strips them.

No conflict markers may survive anywhere. `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and
`>>>>>>>` in a committed file is the most embarrassing diff there is.

@keys
| `/^<<<` | find the next conflict marker |
| `dd` `4dd` | delete the marker line, or the side you are not keeping |
| `cc` | replace a line — a `=======` becomes the blank line between two kept blocks |
| `:g/^[<=>]\{7}/d` | delete every marker in the file at once |
| `]c` `[c` | the change-to-change motion, if you diff the sides instead |
| `:wn` | write and move to the next file |

@hints
Work top to bottom in each file: `<<<<<<<` goes, then whatever you do
with `=======`, then `>>>>>>>`.
--
In the provider you are keeping both sides, so the `=======` line is
not deleted — it is *replaced* by the blank line that separates the two
blocks. `cc` then `Esc` does that in three keystrokes.
--
In the entity you are dropping four lines in one go: the `=======`, the
two lines of this branch's version, and the `>>>>>>>`. Count them and
use `dd` with a count.
--
`:g/^[<=>]\{7}/d` is the quick way to strip markers when you have
already decided both sides stay exactly as they are. It is the wrong
tool for the entity, where a whole side has to go with them.

@solution
    /^<<< <Enter> dd   /^=== <Enter> cc <Esc>   /^>>> <Enter> dd   :wn<Enter>
    /^<<< <Enter> dd   /^=== <Enter> 4dd   :wn<Enter>
    j o Kept the shipped guard from main and the refund window here.<Enter> <Esc>
    :wqa<Enter>

Three things to take from this.

**A conflict is three markers and two blocks.** Once you see it that
way, resolving is deleting lines and occasionally replacing one. The
three-way merge editor with its four panes is a picture of the same
thing, and most of the time the picture is slower than `dd`.

**Decide per conflict, not per file.** `git checkout --ours file` and
`--theirs file` take a whole file's worth of side, which is almost never
what you want when both branches were working on the same method. The
names are also backwards from what people expect during a rebase.

**If the two sides are too close to read**, vim's `-d` flag opens a
proper diff of several files, and `git mergetool` with `vimdiff`
configured drops you into a four-window view of local, base, remote and
the result, where `]c`, `do` and `dp` are exactly the keys you drilled
two lessons ago — `:diffget LOCAL` and `:diffget REMOTE` being the
named versions of `do` when there is more than one other buffer.

Last thing, and it is the one that saves careers: after resolving,
read the whole method through once, top to bottom. A merge that
compiles is not the same as a merge that is correct, and the compiler
cannot see that you kept both halves of a guard in the wrong order.

